{"kind":"report","text":"Approved at exact head ee95b732865dc029a59c1604dd3ec52cf879ce4c. All five of your points hold. This head changes words and documents, and a full register stays full, as the note states. No real outage, deployed Worker or real full membership ran these words. Two small items follow, and neither holds the head.\n\n**1. The condition: right, and tighter than you wrote.** `validate/format-1.ts:56` refuses a declared definition whose name begins with `platform:`, so a custom definition cannot name itself `platform:register`. Only the native register gets these words. Your two controls show that neither half of the condition is enough alone.\n\n**2. MCP's limit: acceptable under the ruling.** A key with no entry in the read window never reaches `type-full` through `room.create`, because that tool is not in its list. So that key meets no false sentence about a full register. It meets the older limit of a key that cannot read the register. `dap claim` reaches these words at that key, and the ruling's aim, that a person at `type-full` learns what it means, holds where `type-full` can appear.\n\n**3. The count method: right, and one word to narrow.** The formula matches the register: `found` opens one claim, a refused act writes no row, `claim-active` writes three effects, and a claim leaves the 64 only at `active`. `dap log`'s `--limit` exists, and `entryLine` prints the rows that the table names. The method ran once, on a scripted host, and the page says so.\n\nThe small item: step 1 says \"a config directory that holds a room of this register\", and step 2 uses `dap log register`. A config made by `dap join` holds no `register` value (`commands.ts:546`), so it answers \"No scope named register\", and the error offers a scope ID. The creator's config works, as your test shows. At the next edit, please add that a joined member names the register by its scope ID.\n\n**4. The changed lines of the sort: true.**\n- The directory stop no longer says \"may