ble file.\n- **Unit 1 is not sealed.** `2026-11-01-failed-claims-words/` has 23 writable files, including its `SHA256SUMS`, though the note calls it sealed. Its digests match. Please run `chmod -R a-w` on it. That changes no head, and I need no new filing for it.\n- The gate at 1db050c6 (exit 0, with 3515 and 1010 tests) ran on tree d5863a0b, the same tree as the failed gate at 4c0f871f. That failure has no failed test, and it is an unhandled `EnvironmentTeardownError`. A pass on the same tree does not explain that failure, and your note says so. 2f31daaa owns it.\n- The red runs and controls ran before the merges, as stated. The head after 1db050c6 changes only the note.\n- I ran `failed-claims-close-register.defect.scope.test.ts` and `failed-claim-words.scope.test.ts` (22 passed), `errors.test.ts` (18 passed), `nothing-sent.test.ts` (8 passed), `security-entry-points` (10 pass), check-notes (0 warnings), check-doc-links (1001, 0 broken) and check-commit-messages (11, 0 flagged). The worktree is clean. I did not rerun the controls, and I sampled the 647-line test by its case names and the cases that the note cites.\n\n**Architecture.** `ClaimSteps` lets the page and the command say different next steps through one `claim()`, and the browser boundary gains one type and no value. No state, count or definition changes.\n\n**Security.** No authority changes. `docs/security/` now states a real availability limit, including that under `open` one key can fill every place. That stays open until option A, and the planner has ruled on it.\n\n**Simplification.** `REGISTER_FULL` exists in both `command-support.ts` and `errors.ts`, because each is framed for its own surface. Its meaning is the same, and each is held by a test. I would leave that.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:24143d601619df06598cae57d4f766ee2eea806c","binding":"assigned","head":"ee95b732865dc029a59c1604dd3ec52cf879ce4c","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589b